{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 2, :comments ["<b>That you may fear HaShem your Elohim</b>: He says that HaShem commanded to teach you the Torah and the commandment and the statutes and the judgments, to do them in the land, in order that you may fear HaShem your Elohim — for the essence of the entire commandment is fear of HaShem. This is the opinion of R[abbi] E[li'ezer]. But it does not seem correct in my eyes that <b>that you may fear</b> and <b>that your days may be prolonged</b> should both be the reason for the commandments — one being the reason, that you may fear, and the second being their reward, that your days may be prolonged. Rather, he says that HaShem commanded me that you should learn the commandments and do them, in order that you may merit and fear HaShem your Elohim, to keep His commandments — you and your son and your son's son — all the days of a person's being upon the face of the earth forever. For through the performance of the commandments you will merit that you shall have children who fear HaShem, and they shall stand upon the face of the earth forever, and your seed and your name shall stand before Him all the days, and that your days may be prolonged in the land that is the heritage of HaShem. And behold, all of this is for your good, for HaShem desires to justify you and to grant you merit."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 6, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T02:45:57.617620092Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}